    "body": "When building a command-line interface with `click.option(..., multiple=True)`, the auto-generated usage string does not visually signal to the user that the option can be repeated. For instance, an option --foo=FOO looks identical whether it accepts single or multiple arguments. This is different from the common convention for multi-value items in shell interfaces often suggests adding an ellipsis like `--foo=FOO...`\n\nExpected behavior if option \"foo\" has `multiple=True`:\n```\nUsage: script.py [OPTIONS]\n\nOptions:\n  --foo TEXT...  A list of foo strings.\n  --help         Show this message and exit.\n```\n",
